# 065 · Journals and a research corpus from every campaign

**Status:** `VERIFIED` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Running campaigns

## What

Every agent writes down what it learns. Each campaign builds a corpus from that, which can go into a knowledge base so that later campaigns start from past research.

- **Journals are field notes:** the workers, the planner and the advisors keep notes as they work, across the whole system:
  - observations;
  - key decisions and why;
  - surprises;
  - open questions;
  - lessons.

  Each note is short, has its kind, and names what it is about (an experiment, an attempt, a source, a round). What was tried and its result are already in the experiment log ([064](064-experiment-queue.goal)), so the journal doesn't repeat them. The harness gives each agent the tool to write a note and keeps the notes in the run's ledger with who wrote them and when. Agents never edit another agent's notes.
- **Research notes:** in every mode, not only research mode, agents can record sources they read, claims with their quotes, and notes. The run's folder ([062](062-research-folder.goal)) holds them, built by the harness from its records, and agents search it.
- **Thermite's own knowledge base:** control keeps a knowledge base of its own; nothing outside is required.
  - **A folder per campaign:** each campaign has a folder, scoped to that campaign, that holds everything its runs produce:
    - the research and the raw sources;
    - the notes and the documentation;
    - the trials, the journals and the experiments with their results;
    - the findings and the final reports.

    Each item keeps where it came from: run, agent and time.
  - **Read access:** a campaign reads its own folder. The owner can let it read other campaigns' folders, at any time, also after the campaign was made, by changing which folders it can read from. Agents can read and search those folders, and never write to them.
- **From past research:** a new campaign's planner, advisors and workers search the folders their campaign can read, and the queue can start with past experiments drawn from them.
- **Outside knowledge bases, optional:** a GBrain-style brain (or another one) is a sync target and a source of extra outside context, not where the research lives:
  - control can copy campaign folders out to it;
  - a campaign can be allowed to read from it as outside context.

## Why

The owner, on 2026-10-20: "All agents should be writing journals, research notes, etc and building a corpus that then we can extract into the knowledge base... so that we can build up a corpus of past research to inform future campaigns and runs."

Today most of what an agent learns ends with its trial. Only submitted attempts, research claims and the final report outlive it.

## Done when

- [x] Workers, the planner and the advisors write field notes (observations, decisions, surprises, questions, lessons), each with what it is about; the notes are in the ledger with who wrote them and when.
- [x] Agents in every mode can record sources, claims and notes in the run's folder.
- [x] Each campaign has a folder in Thermite's knowledge base with everything its runs produce, each item with its origin; a run's export also holds its part.
- [x] The owner sets, and later changes, which other campaigns' folders a campaign can read; agents can search and read them and never write to them.
- [x] A new campaign's agents can search the folders they may read, and the queue can start from them.
- [ ] Moved to [068](068-mcp-servers-for-agents.goal) (MCP servers for agents, owner, 2026-10-20): an outside knowledge base (such as GBrain) is optional; control can sync folders to it, and a campaign can read from it as outside context.
- [x] The dashboard shows a run's journals beside its experiments.
- [x] Tests cover writing, the export, sending to a knowledge base and searching it.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-20, owner: all agents write journals and research notes; a campaign's corpus goes into the knowledge base to inform later campaigns. This goal follows the experiment queue ([064](064-experiment-queue.goal)).
- 2026-10-20, owner: "Build it" (the mockups of 063 and 064); accepted into v0.1.0, built after 064.
- 2026-10-20: [059](059-knowledge-base-export.goal) (research results into a knowledge base) becomes part of this goal.
- 2026-10-20, owner: "Mockups look great btw, I approve those" (the Journal tab, the Knowledge page, a campaign's knowledge, and Start a run from past research).
- 2026-10-20, owner, on the Journal mockup: the journal is a field note, not a record of what was tried (the experiment log has that): observations, key decisions and the like, kept by the agents throughout the system.
- 2026-10-20, owner, on the first mockups: "gbrain shouldn't be required. thermite should maintain its own kb system. gbrain is more a sync than anything and additional external context brought in." Each campaign keeps everything in its own folder in Thermite's knowledge base; the owner grants a campaign read access to other campaigns' folders, at any time.

- 2026-10-20, owner, on whether control should sign the past research it sends to rigs: "This is probably overkill atm." and "overkill atm, keep it as it is with supervisor passing in." Not signed: the supervisor stays trusted for past research, as it is for the advisors' work.
- 2026-10-20, owner, on how much of a journal a new campaign sees: "everything, keep it simple." Outside knowledge bases become plain MCP tools that a kit names ([068](068-mcp-servers-for-agents.goal)); that replaces this goal's outside knowledge base copies and the teachers' search grant.
- 2026-10-20, release lead, on the box about what a note is about: the box stands. A note whose writer names nothing more specific is recorded as about its run, never with `about` left empty, and the lenient reading stays, so a bad note never loses an advisor's work.
- 2026-10-20, from the approved mockups (the Knowledge page, a campaign's knowledge, and Start a run from past research): grants live in a campaign's Knowledge section and past research in Start a run, not on the New campaign page. So the New campaign page links there and says how to use them for a first run (save without starting, grant, Start a run).
- 2026-10-20: "documentation" in a campaign's folder is the text files the lanes write in their working areas of the run's research folder (UTF-8, up to 1 MiB each); other files stay in the run's folder and its export. A research run's hypotheses go in the folder too.

## Open questions

- Answered 2026-10-20 (see Decisions): outside knowledge bases become plain MCP tools ([068](068-mcp-servers-for-agents.goal)); a new campaign sees every note in the folders it may read.

## Progress

- 2026-10-20: Goal written from the owner's direction.

- 2026-10-20: Built on `v1/build` by three helpers and merged: the rig side, control, and outside knowledge bases. Unit tests cover writing notes, the briefing, research in every mode, the sync into each run, the export, the folders and grants, the pages, MCP, and outside knowledge bases with a fake server. The compose acceptances do not cover it yet. What the docs should say:
  - **Field notes (the journal).**
    - The workers, the planner and the advisors keep short notes as they work. A note is an observation, a decision (and why), a surprise, a question or a lesson, and it can name what it is about: an experiment, an attempt, a source, a claim, a finding, a round, a trial or a message.
    - A note is not a record of what was tried: the experiment log has that.
    - Notes are kept in the run's ledger with who wrote them and when (event `journal_note`). Nobody edits or deletes a note.
    - Writers:
      - OpenCode and Claude Code use `thermite-trial note --kind KIND [--about TYPE:ID] TEXT`; Pi has a `note` tool, in code and research trials.
      - The planner's answer and the advisors' answers can carry notes. The supervisor posts an advisor's notes after its work is posted.
    - What each agent sees: a worker's brief has its lane's newest notes and the run's newest decisions and lessons from others; the planner and the teacher pair see the run's newest notes. These are windows on the notes, not limits on writing. Notes are shown to agents as data, not instructions.
  - **Research in every mode.** Workers in hill-climb and goal runs can also fetch sources, read them, search the run's sources, and record claims and notes in the run's folder, as research workers do (`thermite-trial` commands, or Pi's tools). Findings, hypotheses and the report stay in research runs. A research note is its own event (`research_note`), apart from field notes.
  - **The run's Journal tab** (between Experiments and Attempts) shows the field notes, newest first, with filters by writer and by kind and a word filter. What a note is about links to the attempt or the experiment. Research notes are in a side card, with the run's counts and its campaign's folder.
  - **Thermite's knowledge base.** Control keeps a folder for each campaign with everything its runs record: runs, experiments with their results, attempts with their verdicts, field notes and research notes, sources, claims, findings and reports. Each item keeps its campaign, rig, run, who wrote it and when. GBrain or any other outside system is not needed.
  - **The Knowledge page** (sidebar: Knowledge) lists every folder with its counts and runs, who reads it and what it reads, and searches the folders by words, kind and folder. Each result shows where it came from.
  - **A campaign's Knowledge section** shows the folder's counts, "Can read from" (the folders this campaign's agents may read, each with Remove, and Add), and "Who reads" it. The owner can change grants at any time.
  - **How agents read past research.** A run's supervisor asks control every few minutes (`THERMITE_KB_SYNC_SECONDS`, 180) for new and changed items of the folders its campaign may read: its own campaign's other runs, and the granted folders. The harness writes them into the run's research folder under `knowledge/` (one Markdown file per item, with where it came from, and a `knowledge/README.md`). Workers search it there. The planner and the teacher pair get one line saying what it holds; the auditor and the review panel get the briefing only. Control serves a run's sync only for a run it assigned to that rig, of the campaign it assigned. A revoked folder is removed from the run's `knowledge/` at the next sync. Agents never write to any folder: only the harness's records do.
  - **Start a run: From past research.** Search the folders the campaign can read and tick items:
    - ticked experiments join the new run's queue, after any copied untried ones, each saying where it came from and what result it gave;
    - ticked findings, claims, notes and reports become the run's **briefing**: background every agent's brief shows, marked as data, with where each item came from.

    A run without agents takes neither: the dashboard says what it left out, and the harness refuses them from the API, MCP or the CLI. Over MCP, control takes a briefing item's words and a copied experiment's words from its own records, never from the caller; the owner's REST API and `thermite command` send them as given.
  - **MCP:**
    - `kb_folders` and `kb_search {q, campaigns?, kinds?, limit?}`, for the owner and the operator (an agent's or a reader's token sees no folder);
    - `kb_grant` and `kb_revoke {campaign, reads}`, for the owner and the operator;
    - `assign_campaign` takes `briefing: [{text, from: {campaign, run, kind, ref}}]`.
  - **Outside knowledge bases (optional).** In Settings › Providers, add one (an MCP URL over https, or http only for localhost; its token; its search tool and, to copy into it, its write tool), with a check of its tools. The token is kept sealed and never shown again.
    - On a campaign's page, switch on "copy to <name>": control writes each new or changed item of the folder as a page, every `THERMITE_OUTSIDE_KB_SYNC_SECONDS` (300; 0 turns the timer off) and with Sync now. Without a write tool there is no copy.
    - "Let its advisors search it" gives the campaign's teacher pair (on API-key or ChatGPT-subscription routes; not Claude Code or command-line advisors) that knowledge base's search as outside context; the auditor never gets it. Control hands the token only to rigs it assigned a granted campaign, and audits each change of what a rig gets (`outside_kb_handed`). Teachers with an open web fetch get no knowledge-base search, and no past research or briefing in their brief or its tool. A run's items are filed under the campaign control assigned it; a rig cannot file items into a campaign it was not given. Over MCP, a copied experiment takes control's record only from a folder the new run's campaign may read.
    - Workers do not reach outside knowledge bases.
  - **The export.** Each export holds the journal (`journal.jsonl`), the research notes and the briefing. A finished hill-climb or goal run that recorded sources, claims or notes also exports its folder, without `knowledge/`.
  - **Not built:**
    - REST or MCP tools for outside knowledge bases;
    - workers reading outside knowledge bases;
    - a search tool over `knowledge/` for the planner and the advisors;
    - a choice of how much of the journal a new campaign sees (open question above).

- 2026-10-20: Fixes after the docs review: past research and outside tokens follow control's own assignments; items are filed by control's assignment; copies and briefings over MCP keep to grants; open-fetch teachers get no other campaign's records; the knowledge base's MCP tools are the owner's and the operator's. Then checked (full `npm run check`, all seven compose acceptances, and again after the fixes) and deployed on the maintainers' control and rigs (ledger migration 15). The pages are on the docs site. Still open: the acceptance tests do not drive the knowledge base, and the items synced to a rig are not signed by control (the supervisor is trusted for them, as for the advisors' work).

- 2026-10-20: Each box checked against the code, the tests and the pages. The outside knowledge base box moved to 068. Four boxes are met: research notes in every mode, the grants (the harness owns the `knowledge/` files and rebuilds them from its records), the Journal tab, and the unit tests. Three are not met yet:
  - **What a note is about.** A note may leave it out: `about` is optional for the workers, the planner and the advisors, and the pages say a note "can" name it. The box says each note names it. Either the box or the code changes; that is the owner's choice.
  - **Everything a run produces.** A campaign's folder has no trials and no documentation. A source item holds only its address and domain, not the text that was read. The kind `run` exists, but no item of that kind is written: runs are listed apart.
  - **Past research for a new campaign.**
    - The planner and the advisors cannot search the folders: they get one line about `knowledge/`.
    - The New campaign page offers no grants and no past research. So a new campaign's first run reads other campaigns' folders only after a grant, and only a later run (Start a run) can start its queue from them.

  The explanation page's list of parts not built named two that later decisions settled. It now names the knowledge-base search for the planner and the advisors, and links goal 068 for outside knowledge bases.

- 2026-10-20: The three open boxes built, each with its pages, on branch `v010/065-journals-corpus`:
  - **What a note is about.** `about` takes `run`; a note that names nothing is recorded as about its run, and a planner's or advisor's note about a record the run lacks is kept as about the run (lenient as before). Ledger migration 16 rebuilds `journal_note` with `about` required; older notes read as about their run on the dashboard, in the knowledge base and in the export.
  - **Everything a run produces.** One shared reading of events into items (`core/kb-index.ts`), used by control and by the harness. New items: `run` (campaign, version, mode, seed, direction, agents, briefing, then status and best so far), `trial` (lane, round, direction, focus, then how it ended), `doc` (a lane's text file, from a new `research_doc` event at the trial's end) and `hypothesis` (a new `hypothesis_added` event). A source's item holds the text that was read (`source_fetched` carries it, cut to 20,000 characters; the dashboard reads events without it). Each item keeps its run, its writer and its time. Each export writes `knowledge.jsonl`, the items its events added or changed, which the supervisor pushes to `knowledge/<export>.jsonl`; a test checks they equal what control files from the same events. After an upgrade, control reads the events it had indexed once more for the new kinds, so earlier runs get their run and trial items.
  - **Search for the planner and the advisors.** The harness searches its copy of a run's `knowledge/` (read only, marked as data). The planner in Pi has `search_past_research`; in OpenCode or Claude Code it reads the folder's files with its own read tools (Claude Code: `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob` only). The teacher pair, the auditor and each panelist get the same tool through `GET /v1/knowledge`; in Claude Code over MCP on localhost, served by the supervisor for the one call; a teacher with an open web fetch gets none.
  - **New campaign.** The approved mockups show grants and past research on the campaign's page and in Start a run only, so **Review and start** now has a **Past research** note that links to **Knowledge** and gives the steps.
  - Unit tests cover each part; typecheck, lint, the docs build and check, and the public check pass. The compose acceptances were not run on this branch and do not drive the knowledge base yet. A rig must run the new images for past research to sync once control holds the new kinds of item.

